Patient Details

Patient: 45-year-old female

Chief Complaint: Acute onset of widespread skin rash, facial swelling, and mild itching.

Relevant Medical History: No prior drug allergies, chronic hypertension managed with Lisinopril for 3 years.

Timeline & Medication History

Day 1: Diagnosed with a mild upper respiratory tract infection. Prescribed Amoxicillin 500 mg orally three times daily.

Day 1–3: Tolerated Amoxicillin well with initial improvement in respiratory symptoms. Continued regular daily dosage of Lisinopril and occasional Paracetamol for fever.

Day 5: Developed diffuse maculopapular rash across the torso and arms, along with localized periorbital edema (swelling around the eyes).

Day 6: Presented to the clinic; Amoxicillin was discontinued immediately. Antihistamines were prescribed.

Day 8: Skin rash and swelling significantly resolved following drug withdrawal.

Case Analysis Breakdown

Suspected Drug: Amoxicillin.

Reaction Type: Type I / Type IV Hypersensitivity Reaction (Adverse Drug Reaction).

Causality Rationale:

Temporal Relationship: The reaction began 5 days after initiating Amoxicillin, which aligns with standard drug-induced cutaneous hypersensitivity onset windows.

De-challenge: Symptoms rapidly improved after stopping Amoxicillin.

Alternative Causes: Patient has taken Lisinopril for 3 years without prior reaction, making it an unlikely trigger for this acute event. Paracetamol was taken sporadically without prior issues.
